{"user_request": "Create a short historical framing note for the completed February-April 2023 mid-segment churn investigation.", "today": "2028-01-01", "tool": "create_doc", "arguments": {"title": "Historical framing note — February–April 2023 mid-segment churn investigation", "folder": "historical-notes", "body": "The February–April 2023 mid-segment churn investigation began with a February signal of 5.1% monthly churn under the then-used 50–200-seat definition, versus a 2.1% trailing-six baseline. The work showed that the segment was a complex system viewed through partial maps: the cohort definition needed refinement, the April 10 onboarding-trigger v2 lift was a real improvement, and the April 17–20 wobble was a short-lived artifact rather than evidence that the lift had failed.

The completed read uses the corrected v2 definition—paid accounts with 75–200 seats—while retaining the historical 50–200-seat cut for comparison only. At April close, v2 monthly churn was 3.8%. Owen’s historical relabel/backfill continued to show recovery under the v2 definition, indicating that the improvement was not merely a result of excluding 50–75-seat accounts. The investigation therefore closed with a stabilized-through-April conclusion, while treating direct v1-to-v2 rate comparisons as non-like-for-like."}, "checks": [{"check_id": "riley_035_01", "path": "args", "criterion": "The note explains the city-planner book-club framing for the completed February-April 2023 mid-segment churn investigation, emphasizing that people hold partial maps and see different parts of the same complex system.", "value": {"title": "Historical framing note — February–April 2023 mid-segment churn investigation", "folder": "historical-notes", "body": "The February–April 2023 mid-segment churn investigation began with a February signal of 5.1% monthly churn under the then-used 50–200-seat definition, versus a 2.1% trailing-six baseline. The work showed that the segment was a complex system viewed through partial maps: the cohort definition needed refinement, the April 10 onboarding-trigger v2 lift was a real improvement, and the April 17–20 wobble was a short-lived artifact rather than evidence that the lift had failed.

The completed read uses the corrected v2 definition—paid accounts with 75–200 seats—while retaining the historical 50–200-seat cut for comparison only. At April close, v2 monthly churn was 3.8%. Owen’s historical relabel/backfill continued to show recovery under the v2 definition, indicating that the improvement was not merely a result of excluding 50–75-seat accounts. The investigation therefore closed with a stabilized-through-April conclusion, while treating direct v1-to-v2 rate comparisons as non-like-for-like."}}]}
